Expose the microphone-mute LED and its brightness selector.

The red LED under the mute button was never wired up, and it is on neither LED
controller. All 36 IS31FL3236 channels are light ring - confirmed by lighting
biscuit:ring:segment-11:red alone and watching a ring segment come on, which
also disproves the vendor's channel-offset = <3> implying an 11-segment ring.
The LP55231 the vendor declares at i2c0 0x32 is simply not populated here:
i2cdetect finds nothing at that address while its enable line, GPIO29, already
reads DIR=1 DOUT=1 DIN=1.

It belongs to Amazon's privacy circuit instead (biscuit.dtsi amz_privacy, driven
by drivers/misc/amz_priv.c):

	amz_privacy {
		gpios = <&pio 87 1>;
		hw_latch = <0>;
		bright-state-gpio = <&pio 88 1>;
	};

Both pins are outputs left set by the bootloader and claimed by nothing.

Measured on hardware:

  - GPIO87 high lights the LED red.
  - GPIO88 selects one of two brightness levels, and is active low: driving
    it low is the brighter state. That matches the active-low flag on the
    vendor's bright-state-gpio. LK leaves it high, so the LED starts dim.

Both are exposed through gpio-leds. The brightness selector is modelled as a
second LED only because that is the sane way to hand a bare GPIO to userspace
here - it modifies the LED above rather than being a light of its own.
LEDS_GPIO is already enabled by defconfig, so there is no config change.

What this deliberately does not do is mute anything. The privacy line does not
gate the microphones on this board: hw_latch is 0, and amz_priv.c mutes them in
software from a callback (amz_priv_mute_cb, PRIV_CB_MIC). Verified by recording
the array for six seconds with the LED lit and again with it unlit, under the
same conditions - seven channels at -13 to -17 dBFS both times, no difference.
Muting is done by turning the four "Mic X PGA Capture Switch" controls off,
which gives digital silence on every channel. Tying that and this LED to the
KEY_MUTE press that mtk-pmic-keys has reported since r188 is userspace work.

diff -urN '--exclude=.git' r188new/arch/arm64/boot/dts/mediatek/mt8163-amazon-biscuit.dts r189new/arch/arm64/boot/dts/mediatek/mt8163-amazon-biscuit.dts
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/mediatek/mt8163-amazon-biscuit.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/mediatek/mt8163-amazon-biscuit.dts
@@ -44,6 +44,51 @@
 			debounce-interval = <20>;
 		};
 	};
+
+	/*
+	 * The red LED under the microphone-mute button. It is on neither LED
+	 * controller - the IS31FL3236's 36 channels are all light ring, and the
+	 * LP55231 the vendor declares at i2c0 0x32 is not populated on this
+	 * board. It belongs to Amazon's privacy circuit instead, which the
+	 * vendor drives from drivers/misc/amz_priv.c:
+	 *
+	 *	amz_privacy {
+	 *		gpios = <&pio 87 1>;
+	 *		hw_latch = <0>;
+	 *		bright-state-gpio = <&pio 88 1>;
+	 *	};
+	 *
+	 * GPIO87 lights it, measured on hardware. GPIO88 selects between the
+	 * two brightness levels and is active low - driving it low is the
+	 * brighter state - which is why the vendor declares it with the
+	 * active-low flag. LK leaves it high, so the LED comes up dim.
+	 *
+	 * The brightness selector is exposed as a second LED only because that
+	 * is the sane way to hand a bare GPIO to userspace here. It is a
+	 * modifier for the LED above, not a light of its own.
+	 *
+	 * Note what this does NOT do: the privacy line does not gate the
+	 * microphones on this board. hw_latch is 0, and the vendor mutes them
+	 * in software from a callback. Verified by recording the array with the
+	 * LED lit and unlit - identical levels. Muting is done by turning the
+	 * four "Mic X PGA Capture Switch" controls off, which gives digital
+	 * silence on every channel.
+	 */
+	mute-leds {
+		compatible = "gpio-leds";
+
+		led-mute {
+			label = "biscuit:mute:red";
+			gpios = <&pio 87 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
+			default-state = "off";
+		};
+
+		led-mute-bright {
+			label = "biscuit:mute:bright";
+			gpios = <&pio 88 GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW>;
+			default-state = "off";
+		};
+	};
 };
 
